1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,057
In the last few weeks, we've been looking at various aspects of the social

2
00:00:04,057 --> 00:00:04,841
history of London,

3
00:00:04,841 --> 00:00:08,577
and this morning we're continuing with a look at life in the area called

4
00:00:08,577 --> 00:00:09,280
the East End.

5
00:00:09,280 --> 00:00:12,114
I'll start with a brief history of the district,

6
00:00:12,114 --> 00:00:15,490
and then focus on life in the first half of the twentieth century.

7
00:00:15,490 --> 00:00:19,538
Back in the first to the fourth centuries AD,

8
00:00:19,538 --> 00:00:25,669
when the Romans controlled England, London grew into a town of 45,000 people,

9
00:00:25,669 --> 00:00:28,955
and what's now the East End-the area by the river Thames,

10
00:00:28,955 --> 00:00:34,185
and along the road heading north-east from London to the coast-consisted of farmland with crops

11
00:00:34,185 --> 00:00:36,670
and livestock which helped to feed that population.

12
00:00:36,670 --> 00:00:41,542
The Romans left in 410, at the beginning of the fifth century,

13
00:00:41,542 --> 00:00:47,737
and from then onwards the country suffered a series of invasions by tribes from present-day Germany

14
00:00:47,737 --> 00:00:52,789
and Denmark, the Angles, Saxons and Jutes, many of whom settled in the East

15
00:00:52,789 --> 00:00:53,070
End.

16
00:00:53,070 --> 00:00:58,565
The technology they introduced meant that metal and leather goods were produced there for

17
00:00:58,565 --> 00:00:59,330
the first time.

18
00:00:59,330 --> 00:01:04,435
And as the East End was by the river, ships could transport goods between there

19
00:01:04,435 --> 00:01:05,540
and foreign markets.

20
00:01:05,540 --> 00:01:12,061
In the eleventh century, in 1066 to be precise, the Normans conquered England,

21
00:01:12,061 --> 00:01:16,094
and during the next few centuries London became one of the most powerful

22
00:01:16,094 --> 00:01:17,860
and prosperous cities in Europe.

23
00:01:17,860 --> 00:01:20,365
The East End benefited from this,

24
00:01:20,365 --> 00:01:25,596
and because there were fewer restrictions there than in the city itself, plenty of

25
00:01:25,596 --> 00:01:29,945
newcomers settled there from abroad, bringing their skills as workers, merchants

26
00:01:29,945 --> 00:01:32,610
or money-lenders during the next few hundred years.

27
00:01:32,610 --> 00:01:40,098
In the sixteenth century the first dock was dug where ships were constructed, eventually

28
00:01:40,098 --> 00:01:43,250
making the East End the focus of massive international trade.

29
00:01:43,250 --> 00:01:45,700
And in the late sixteenth century,

30
00:01:45,700 --> 00:01:50,560
when much of the rest of England was suffering economically, a lot of agricultural

31
00:01:50,560 --> 00:01:53,090
workers came to the East End to look for alternative work.

32
00:01:53,090 --> 00:01:59,297
In the seventeenth century, the East End was still a series of separate, semi-rural

33
00:01:59,297 --> 00:01:59,960
settlements.

34
00:01:59,960 --> 00:02:02,232
There was a shortage of accommodation,

35
00:02:02,232 --> 00:02:06,856
so marshland was drained and built on to house the large numbers of people

36
00:02:06,856 --> 00:02:07,620
now living there.

37
00:02:07,620 --> 00:02:13,185
By the nineteenth century London was the busiest port in the world,

38
00:02:13,185 --> 00:02:16,680
and this became the main source of employment in the East End.

39
00:02:16,680 --> 00:02:20,573
Those who could afford to live in more pleasant surroundings moved out,

40
00:02:20,573 --> 00:02:25,389
and the area became one where the vast majority of people lived in extreme

41
00:02:25,389 --> 00:02:25,950
poverty,

42
00:02:25,950 --> 00:02:28,840
and suffered from appalling sanitary conditions.

43
00:02:28,840 --> 00:02:33,220
That brief outline takes us to the beginning of the twentieth century,

44
00:02:33,220 --> 00:02:35,390
and now we'll turn to housing.

45
00:02:35,390 --> 00:02:41,092
At the beginning of the century, living conditions for the majority of working people

46
00:02:41,092 --> 00:02:43,140
in East London were very basic indeed.

47
00:02:43,140 --> 00:02:48,082
Houses were crowded closely together and usually very badly built,

48
00:02:48,082 --> 00:02:49,790
because there was no regulation.

49
00:02:49,790 --> 00:02:54,032
But the poor and needy were attracted by the possibility of work,

50
00:02:54,032 --> 00:02:55,680
and they had to be housed.

51
00:02:55,680 --> 00:03:01,312
It was the availability, rather than the condition, of the housing that was the major concern for tenants

52
00:03:01,312 --> 00:03:02,700
and landlords alike.

53
00:03:02,700 --> 00:03:06,316
Few houses had electricity at this time,

54
00:03:06,316 --> 00:03:10,494
so other sources of power were used, like coal for the fires which heated

55
00:03:10,494 --> 00:03:11,800
perhaps just one room.

56
00:03:11,800 --> 00:03:16,610
Of course, the smoke from these contributed a great deal to the air pollution

57
00:03:16,610 --> 00:03:18,300
for which London used to be famous.

58
00:03:18,300 --> 00:03:24,461
A tiny, damp, unhealthy house like this might well be occupied by two full

59
00:03:24,461 --> 00:03:30,000
families, possibly including several children, grandparents, aunts and uncles.

60
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:36,615
Now, before I go on to health implications of this way of life, I'll say something about food

61
00:03:36,615 --> 00:03:37,640
and nutrition.
